Question 1 (10 points) 
In the bulls and cows game, a player tries to discover a secret code by trial and error. 
In our implementation, the secret code consists of 4 digits. Each digit is in the range [0-3]. 

Write the BullsAndCows subroutine in ARM assembly language. You must follow this 
prototype: 

int BullsAndCows(int guess[4], int secret[4], int guessFrequency[4], int secretFrequency[4]); 


The four parameters are addresses of arrays containing four integers. 
In the Reset_Handler, before calling the subroutine, you can initialize the elements of guess 
(the player’s try) and secret (the code to be discovered) with values of your choice. Instead, all the 
elements of guessFrequency and secretFrequency must be initialized to zero. 
The BullsAndCows subroutine computes: 
- the bulls: the number of digits that appear in both arrays (guess and secret) in the same 
position; 
- the cows: the number of digits in guess that are not bulls, but match the elements in secret in 
another position; 

Example: guess = [0, 1, 2, 3]; secret = [1, 2, 2, 0]. 
There is one bull (digit 2 in the third position) and two cows (digits 0 and 1). 

The subroutine implements the following pseudocode: 
bulls = 0; 
cows = 0; 
for (i = 0; i < 4; i ++) { 
 if (guess[i] == secret[i]) 
  bulls ++; 
 else { 
  guessFrequency[guess[i]] ++; 
  secretFrequency[secret[i]] ++; 
 } 
} 
for (i = 0; i < 4; i ++) 
 cows += min(guessFrequency[i], secretFrequency[i]); 
return (2bulls -1) << 4 + (2cows -1) 

In the example above, at the end of the procedure we have: 
guessFrequency = [1, 1, 0, 1] 
secretFrequency = [1, 1, 1, 0] 
bulls = 1 
cows = 2 
(2bulls – 1) = 1 
(2bulls – 1) << 4 = 16 
(2cows – 1) = 3 
The subroutine returns 19.

